


She faced the camera. “Blue marker one. Starting solo control. One question, thirty seconds. I’ll log any sound changes whether they follow a question or an ordinary statement.”




She said it before asking the first question so the procedure would be on the recording before anything happened.




She asked, “Can you make one sound near this marker?”




She started the stopwatch.




Crickets continued at several distances. Water remained audible behind the rise. At nine seconds, a branch released a light scatter of leaves somewhere above her. At twenty-two, a frog called twice from the river side. No other frog joined it.




At thirty seconds, she wrote: two frog notes; one leaf movement; no distinct local response.




She waited another thirty seconds without speaking. The same frog called once at the beginning of the interval. Crickets weakened across the slope and returned in uneven patches.




Maren recorded the control interval.




Her radio clicked.




“White marker?” she said.




Static. Then Jonah: “Three.”




“You skipped the scheduled check.”




“I was moving.”




“Stop at three.”




A pause. “Stopped.”




“Confirm white marker three.”




“White three.”




Maren looked at her watch. The reply had come forty seconds late.




“Next contact at eleven forty-three.”




“Copy.”




She wrote the missed interval on the route sheet rather than asking what had delayed him.




For the second prompt, she kept her voice at the same volume. “If anything is close enough to hear me, make one sound.”




Six seconds passed.




A frog called from the low ground.




A second note followed from farther right. Then several frogs began at once near the river, low pulses overlapping until Maren could no longer separate individuals. Crickets on the slope continued above them.




The muscles along her forearms tightened. She had heard the chorus begin and end several times during the route walk. None of those cycles had required a question. But the first call still came during the thirty seconds she had left open for an answer.




She watched the stopwatch reach thirty.




“Frog chorus began six seconds after prompt,” she said. “Direction: river side. Other insects continued. Cause not established.”




The chorus diminished. One frog continued alone, then stopped.




Maren waited through another silent interval. At eleven seconds she said, “Camera battery sixty-three percent.”




A frog called immediately after the word percent.




She looked toward the dark below the trail before she wrote it down.




“Control statement followed by one frog note at less than one second,” she said. “This was not a question.”




The same timing had followed an ordinary statement. Maren could no longer treat the earlier sequence as unusual, even though she still felt the jolt of hearing the frog call before her mouth had fully closed.




Her radio remained quiet.




At eleven forty-three, she pressed transmit. “Blue one. Scheduled contact.”




No reply.




She waited five seconds.




“Jonah, state marker.”




Static moved through the speaker. Beneath it she heard a short scrape and what might have been his breath.




“White route,” he said.




“Marker number.”




The transmission ended.




Maren pressed again. “State the number.”




This time his reply came quickly. “Four.”




The official map showed only three numbered posts before the white route curved back toward the northern loop. She lifted the plastic sleeve and checked. The number four belonged to the return branch beyond the river overlook, farther than he could have reached without continuing past their agreed stopping point.




“Hold position at four,” she said. “Do not advance.”




“I heard something ahead.”




“Hold at the marker.”




No answer.




“Jonah.”




“Copy.”




His voice had dropped. She could not tell whether he was trying to remain quiet or had moved the radio farther from his mouth.




Maren logged the position conflict and looked down the blue trail. The first marker stood less than a minute from the junction. She could return, call him from there, and end the separation. The timer showed about eight minutes remaining. He had broken the distance limit but still claimed a numbered marker on the permitted route.




She left the camera on the tripod.




For the next prompt, she moved three steps from the camera and kept both hands visible in frame. “Can you repeat the sound that followed my last question?”




The slope remained loud with crickets.




At four seconds, one stopped close to the trail.




At eight, the cluster farther uphill stopped.




Crickets fell quiet in patches, not all at once. Water and distant frogs remained. Maren could identify no abrupt boundary and no single source. As more crickets stopped, the rustle of her clothes became more noticeable when she breathed.




She counted the remaining seconds in her head.




At twenty-seven, a frog called from below.




At twenty-eight, another joined it.




At thirty, the low ground broke into a dense chorus.




Maren did not speak until the stopwatch passed thirty-one.




“Second question followed by staggered cricket reduction and frog chorus beginning twenty-seven seconds after prompt. Similar environmental cycles occurred without prompts.”




By stating the comparison aloud, she kept the ordinary alternatives in the record. Her hands did not stop shaking.




She pressed the radio. “Blue one. Contact.”




Jonah answered after three seconds. “I can hear you.”




“State marker.”




A scrape of brush came through his transmission.




“Still four.”




“Are you on the white trail?”




Another scrape. Then, “Yes.”




He answered too quickly and did not repeat the marker number. Maren lifted the camera from the tripod and aimed down the route toward the junction. The tripod camera had been the only continuous record of the blue control. Moving the camera would end it. Leaving it behind would give her both hands and preserve the frame, but it would also leave no image of whatever happened next.




She returned the camera to the tripod.




“Turn around now,” she said into the radio. “Walk back to the junction. Confirm.”




No response.




Maren waited.




Crickets continued on the slope. Water remained audible below the rise.


“Jonah, confirm return.”




A light moved through the trees below and to her right.




It appeared once between two trunks, vanished, and appeared farther upslope. The white route lay below that line. The beam should have moved laterally along the river loop. Instead it climbed toward the wooded rise and the closed staff path.




Maren took the camera off the tripod.




She zoomed only enough to hold the light between branches. It moved again, higher and farther from the trail. Brush cut across it. For two seconds the beam pointed back through the trees and struck pale leaves in a narrow fan.




“Jonah, stop moving.”




The light stopped.




Her radio clicked.




A close, uneven breath filled the speaker, loud enough to distort.




Then Jonah said something she heard as “found” or “ground.” The final consonant disappeared beneath static.




“Repeat.”




The light swung down and vanished behind the rise.




Maren stepped away from the blue marker. The camera remained recording in her hand, but its frame caught only the trail as she moved.
